{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Aaron saw.</b> That it had a spirit of life in it, as it says ״בְּתַבְנִית שׁוֹר אֹכֵל עֵשֶׂב״ (Psalms 106), and he saw that the deed of Satan had succeeded, and he had no mouth to push them off entirely." "<b>And he built an altar.</b> To push them off." "<b>And he proclaimed: A festival to YHWH tomorrow.</b> And not today — perhaps Moses will come before they serve it; this is its plain sense. And its midrash, in Vayikra Rabbah: Aaron saw many things — he saw Hur, his sister's son, who had rebuked them, and they killed him; and this is ״וַיִּבֶן מִזְבֵּחַ לְפָנָיו״ — וַיָּבֶן מִזָּבוּחַ לְפָנָיו. And further, he saw and said: Better that the blame hang on me and not on them. And further, he saw and said: If they build the altar, this one brings a pebble and that one brings a stone, and their work is done in one stroke; whereas if I build it and I take my time with the work, in the meantime Moses comes." "<b>A festival to YHWH.</b> In his heart it was toward heaven; he was certain that Moses would come and they would serve the Omnipresent."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:07.657244199Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}